These hunting tools have ensured our survival,

4
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:13,000
altered our evolution,

5
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:16,000
and maintained our dominion over the Earth's animals.

6
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:19,000
Today, they are the backbone of a multi-billion dollar

7
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:21,000
sports industry.

8
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:26,000
Now, hunting gear on Modern Marvels.

9
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:41,940
After two million years of evolution, man

10
00:00:41,941 --> 00:00:46,040
stands as the world's greatest predator.

11
00:00:46,041 --> 00:00:49,360
Millennia of developments in hunting technology

12
00:00:49,361 --> 00:00:51,980
have outfitted the modern hunter with every conceivable

13
00:00:51,981 --> 00:00:57,680
advantage, gadget, and gizmo to bag wild game at will.

14
00:00:57,681 --> 00:01:00,420
Take AirScan, a Florida-based company

15
00:01:00,421 --> 00:01:02,420
that scouts new hunting areas by aircraft.

16
00:01:06,180 --> 00:01:10,300
Each plane is equipped with video cameras, infrared,

17
00:01:10,301 --> 00:01:14,520
and multispectral sensors that can count animal herds,

18
00:01:14,521 --> 00:01:17,040
assess tracking routes, and monitor habitat.

19
00:01:21,240 --> 00:01:24,600
The data is provided to hunting clients and gamekeepers

20
00:01:24,601 --> 00:01:29,140
to manage wildlife and target specific animals.

21
00:01:29,141 --> 00:01:31,740
While still employing ancient skills,

22
00:01:31,741 --> 00:01:35,000
today's hunter treads a fine line between sport

23
00:01:35,001 --> 00:01:36,000
and high-tech overkill.

24
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:41,500
The best way to see the selling and celebration

25
00:01:41,501 --> 00:01:43,720
of all this cutting-edge hardware

26
00:01:43,721 --> 00:01:47,200
is at the annual SHOT Show, the largest hunting gear

27
00:01:47,201 --> 00:01:50,800
convention in the world.

28
00:01:50,801 --> 00:01:54,600
Each year, more than 100,000 manufacturers and retailers

29
00:01:54,601 --> 00:01:57,800
stalk this bullseye bazaar for equipment that would have been

30
00:01:57,801 --> 00:02:01,900
unimaginable to their ancient brethren.

31
00:02:01,901 --> 00:02:06,040
One popular appliance is the Stealth Cam, a 24-hour-a-day

32
00:02:06,041 --> 00:02:09,240
digital camera mounted on trees.

33
00:02:09,241 --> 00:02:12,460
A scout can enter an area briefly, post the camera,

34
00:02:12,461 --> 00:02:15,760
and retreat without leaving telltale human sense

35
00:02:15,761 --> 00:02:17,860
that can spook game.

36
00:02:17,861 --> 00:02:19,760
People will buy up to three or four of these things.

37
00:02:19,761 --> 00:02:21,860
Stick them in various places in the areas

38
00:02:21,861 --> 00:02:23,400
that they usually go hunting.

39
00:02:23,401 --> 00:02:27,000
And they can come back a week later, three weeks later.

40
00:02:27,001 --> 00:02:28,800
They get the camera, they develop the film.

41
00:02:28,801 --> 00:02:31,740
If they want to hunt turkey, and they get a bunch of raccoons,

42
00:02:31,741 --> 00:02:34,040
and they know this is not the place for them to be.

43
00:02:34,041 --> 00:02:38,240
The main feature is the PIR sensor with passive infrared.

44
00:02:38,241 --> 00:02:40,160
It's located behind this white shield here.

45
00:02:40,161 --> 00:02:44,060
It uses heat and motion to activate the camera,

46
00:02:44,061 --> 00:02:45,360
to take a picture.

47
00:02:45,361 --> 00:02:47,460
Reason for that is if you did a motion detector,

48
00:02:47,461 --> 00:02:49,400
a tree branch would set it off.

49
00:02:49,401 --> 00:02:51,460
So we have the heat activated as well.

50
00:02:51,461 --> 00:02:54,660
This camera is programmed to take between one and nine

51
00:02:54,661 --> 00:03:00,060
pictures each time the intrusion area is activated.

52
00:03:00,061 --> 00:03:02,860
But if you want to see your quarry in real time,

53
00:03:02,861 --> 00:03:06,820
these rangefinder binoculars fire an invisible laser beam,

54
00:03:06,821 --> 00:03:10,660
which bounces off a target and returns to a receptor inside

55
00:03:10,661 --> 00:03:14,460
the binoculars. If we know the speed of the laser,

56
00:03:14,461 --> 00:03:17,100
which is the speed of light, and if we can calculate the tiny

57
00:03:17,101 --> 00:03:21,700
amount of time elapsing between when it leaves and gets back,

58
00:03:21,800 --> 00:03:24,340
then we multiply the speed of light

59
00:03:24,440 --> 00:03:26,740
by this tiny amount of time, and that's

60
00:03:26,840 --> 00:03:29,040
what gives us the distance.

61
00:03:29,140 --> 00:03:31,240
Software inside the binoculars quickly

62
00:03:31,340 --> 00:03:35,860
computes distances up to 1,100 feet,

63
00:03:35,960 --> 00:03:39,460
which significantly aids tracking, identifying prey,

64
00:03:39,560 --> 00:03:42,460
and shot accuracy.

65
00:03:42,560 --> 00:03:44,860
And for those seeking their inner animal,

66
00:03:44,861 --> 00:03:49,060
There's the PowerMuff quads, an ear device that comes with four

67
00:03:49,061 --> 00:03:52,360
separate high-frequency microphones to amplify the sounds

68
00:03:52,361 --> 00:03:53,360
of nature.

69
00:03:53,361 --> 00:03:56,100
It increases your normal hearing five to eight times.

70
00:03:56,101 --> 00:04:00,300
It lets you hear those distant sounds like turkeys gobbling,

71
00:04:00,301 --> 00:04:02,620
leaves rustling, but it automatically shuts off at the

72
00:04:02,621 --> 00:04:03,920
report of the muzzle blast.

73
00:04:03,921 --> 00:04:06,420
It's great for the shooter on the range because you can hear

74
00:04:06,421 --> 00:04:09,260
the range commands.

75
00:04:09,261 --> 00:04:12,520
Once game is targeted, predators can now have a

76
00:04:12,521 --> 00:04:14,520
record of their time in the field.

77
00:04:18,520 --> 00:04:21,520
Adirondack Optics, a New York manufacturer,

78
00:04:21,521 --> 00:04:24,520
recently introduced a device that gives new meaning to the

79
00:04:24,521 --> 00:04:27,520
word shoot. Very simply what this is,

80
00:04:27,521 --> 00:04:30,520
is an integration of a digital camera with a telescopic rifle

81
00:04:30,521 --> 00:04:33,520
sight, with the idea being it captures a digital photograph

82
00:04:33,521 --> 00:04:37,520
when you discharge your firearm. It also has a manual

83
00:04:37,521 --> 00:04:40,520
shutter release on it, so you can take a digital photograph

84
00:04:40,521 --> 00:04:44,020
it'll photograph with it without discharging your firearm.

85
00:04:44,021 --> 00:04:46,520
Inside here, there's a recoil sensitive switch

86
00:04:46,521 --> 00:04:49,720
called an accelerometer, which senses the recoil.

87
00:04:49,721 --> 00:04:52,520
And through some special software designs,

88
00:04:52,521 --> 00:04:54,220
it reaches back into the memory.

89
00:04:54,221 --> 00:04:56,920
And a few thousandths of a second before the recoil was

90
00:04:56,921 --> 00:04:59,720
sensed, it pulls that image out of the memory.

91
00:04:59,721 --> 00:05:02,820
And that is the actual image of what you saw when you decided

92
00:05:02,821 --> 00:05:04,720
to pull the trigger.

93
00:05:04,721 --> 00:05:07,720
Today's cutting edge hunting gear at the SHOT Show

94
00:05:07,721 --> 00:05:10,120
is a far cry from the rudimentary instruments

95
00:05:10,121 --> 00:05:13,560
by our prehistoric ancestors for their safety, survival,

96
00:05:13,660 --> 00:05:16,260
and sustenance.

97
00:05:16,360 --> 00:05:19,160
Archaeologists believe the first hunting tools were created

98
00:05:19,260 --> 00:05:23,960
about 2.5 million years BC and involved flaking off the edges

99
00:05:24,060 --> 00:05:27,880
of small stones into crude knives.

100
00:05:27,980 --> 00:05:31,380
Mann then found ways to surround animals by using fire

101
00:05:31,480 --> 00:05:34,580
and other herding methods.

102
00:05:34,680 --> 00:05:39,520
Early Mann hunted with his wits and catching animals

103
00:05:39,521 --> 00:05:42,820
often in places where he might have advantage as much

104
00:05:42,920 --> 00:05:47,160
as anything else, in a canyon, driving animals over a cliff,

105
00:05:47,260 --> 00:05:50,360
into a swamp, into water.

106
00:05:50,460 --> 00:05:54,460
Also, you might build a pit and drive them into the pit,

107
00:05:54,560 --> 00:05:56,400
or lure them into the pit with meat

108
00:05:56,500 --> 00:05:58,980
or some other kind of food.

109
00:05:59,080 --> 00:06:02,880
So essentially, you were trying to use the natural features

110
00:06:02,980 --> 00:06:07,380
to be able to entrap the animal with a little human help.

111
00:06:07,381 --> 00:06:10,220
Once the animals were cornered, man then

112
00:06:10,221 --> 00:06:12,880
used his rudimentary weapons for close-in killing

113
00:06:12,881 --> 00:06:13,581
and butchering.

114
00:06:17,820 --> 00:06:22,120
By about 500,000 BC, hand axes began to appear,

115
00:06:22,121 --> 00:06:24,340
which provided the hunter with more leverage

116
00:06:24,341 --> 00:06:26,340
by affixing a blade to the end of a stick.

117
00:06:28,940 --> 00:06:33,780
Around 60,000 BC, spears were invented, using wooden shafts

118
00:06:33,781 --> 00:06:36,980
with points hardened in fire, which were compressed,

119
00:06:36,981 --> 00:06:39,920
cured and dried by the heat.

120
00:06:40,020 --> 00:06:42,420
The weapon gave our ancestors increased thrusting

121
00:06:42,520 --> 00:06:45,220
and throwing power.

122
00:06:45,320 --> 00:06:47,220
The spear gave him great leverage.

123
00:06:47,320 --> 00:06:49,460
It gave him the ability to kill an animal,

124
00:06:49,560 --> 00:06:54,940
because man with his bare hands is a pretty harmless creature.

125
00:06:55,040 --> 00:06:57,140
And so man has to have tools.

126
00:06:57,240 --> 00:06:59,140
And man's ability to work with tools,

127
00:06:59,240 --> 00:07:02,940
this is the way man is able to become an equal and even a

128
00:07:03,040 --> 00:07:05,880
superior to the beasts that have much greater strength

129
00:07:05,881 --> 00:07:06,780
than he does.

130
00:07:06,781 --> 00:07:08,420
There's really no mystery as to why man

131
00:07:08,421 --> 00:07:09,480
developed these hunting tools.

132
00:07:09,481 --> 00:07:11,180
Obviously, when you live with hunger every day,

133
00:07:11,181 --> 00:07:13,820
you're looking for ways to feed yourself.

134
00:07:13,821 --> 00:07:16,020
Hunting tools were also protective tools

135
00:07:16,021 --> 00:07:18,220
against other tribes, against animals.

136
00:07:18,221 --> 00:07:20,060
You've got to keep in mind that there wasn't a great deal

137
00:07:20,061 --> 00:07:22,360
of distance between man and the rest of the food chain

138
00:07:22,361 --> 00:07:23,900
early on.

139
00:07:23,901 --> 00:07:25,560
There was something in human nature

140
00:07:25,561 --> 00:07:28,600
that drew us to eating meat.

141
00:07:28,601 --> 00:07:32,300
As they ate more and more meat, that protein came

142
00:07:32,301 --> 00:07:35,580
and actually fueled growth in brain cells.

143
00:07:35,581 --> 00:07:38,380
So you start to see cranial capacity developing.

144
00:07:38,381 --> 00:07:40,880
And with increased cranial capacity,

145
00:07:40,881 --> 00:07:45,820
you see the tools start to evolve.

146
00:07:45,821 --> 00:07:49,820
With the invention of the bow and arrow around 30,000 BC,

147
00:07:49,821 --> 00:07:52,140
we drastically increased our hunting prowess

148
00:07:52,141 --> 00:07:55,700
by being able to kill at a distance.

149
00:07:55,701 --> 00:07:58,640
Our ancestors could hunt larger game much more safely

150
00:07:58,641 --> 00:08:01,140
and efficiently.

151
00:08:01,141 --> 00:08:05,000
By 19,000 BC, the atlatl, a shortened spear

152
00:08:05,001 --> 00:08:07,240
that was thrown with an attached stick

153
00:08:07,241 --> 00:08:10,140
gave hunters mechanical leverage and faster,

154
00:08:10,141 --> 00:08:12,640
more lethal projectiles than those thrown by hand.

155
00:08:15,440 --> 00:08:17,340
As hunter and gatherer cultures evolved

156
00:08:17,341 --> 00:08:19,400
into more stable agrarian societies

157
00:08:19,401 --> 00:08:22,300
with the planting of seasonal crops,

158
00:08:22,301 --> 00:08:26,140
sport hunting began to appear.

159
00:08:26,141 --> 00:08:29,160
Ancient Egyptian drawings on friezes around tombs

160
00:08:29,161 --> 00:08:33,400
appearing about 1500 BC show rulers hunting foul

161
00:08:33,401 --> 00:08:40,100
with nets, spears, and bowlers, sometimes with the aid of chariots and greyhounds.

162
00:08:40,101 --> 00:08:47,140
The seeds of sport hunting probably go back to the Fertile Crescent and to Egypt,

163
00:08:47,141 --> 00:08:52,960
where people gained control of land and extra resources,

164
00:08:52,961 --> 00:09:00,660
and they were able to use those in ways that created somewhat of differences in caste and class.

165
00:09:00,661 --> 00:09:04,560
The origins of sport hunting are associated with royalty

166
00:09:04,660 --> 00:09:08,800
simply because they did not have to hunt for survival.

167
00:09:08,900 --> 00:09:14,000
Falconry was also a popular pastime of Egyptian noblemen.

168
00:09:14,100 --> 00:09:17,520
They would carry the falcon around on a big,

169
00:09:17,620 --> 00:09:21,320
gauntleted hand, keep a hood over the falcon

170
00:09:21,420 --> 00:09:24,420
so that it wouldn't either be nervous or be distracted.

171
00:09:24,520 --> 00:09:26,060
And then when they would sight the game,

172
00:09:26,160 --> 00:09:28,060
the hood would come off, and they

173
00:09:28,061 --> 00:09:31,100
and they would be unleashed from whatever restraint

174
00:09:31,101 --> 00:09:32,100
they'd be on.

175
00:09:32,101 --> 00:09:36,300
The falcon would go up and capture it in midair.

176
00:09:36,301 --> 00:09:38,900
While falconry remains virtually unchanged

177
00:09:38,901 --> 00:09:41,300
to this day, other ancient weapons

178
00:09:41,301 --> 00:09:45,520
have been completely transformed by technology.

179
00:09:45,521 --> 00:09:48,980
Next, bows and arrows, hunting knives.

180
00:09:48,981 --> 00:09:53,480
For thousands of years, these tools determined our survival.

181
00:09:53,481 --> 00:09:58,060
Now their cutting-edge science is coveted by sportsmen.

182
00:09:58,061 --> 00:10:00,360
Herding and driving animals over cliffs

183
00:10:00,460 --> 00:10:02,760
was an ancient hunting technique.

184
00:10:02,860 --> 00:10:05,360
At the base of a cliff near Lyon, France,

185
00:10:05,460 --> 00:10:08,100
the remains of more than 10,000 wild horses

186
00:10:08,200 --> 00:10:13,000
have been found, dating from around 40,000 BC.

187
00:10:13,100 --> 00:10:15,920
Hunting Gear will return on Modern Marvels.

188
00:10:20,320 --> 00:10:23,620
We now return to Hunting Gear on Modern Marvels.

189
00:10:23,621 --> 00:10:26,620
marvels.

190
00:10:26,621 --> 00:10:30,860
The Cybertech is the state of the art in bow hunting,

191
00:10:30,861 --> 00:10:33,620
a compound bow with a high-strength aluminum

192
00:10:33,621 --> 00:10:37,660
handle and limbs with multiple laminations of carbon fiber

193
00:10:37,661 --> 00:10:40,020
and fiberglass.

194
00:10:40,021 --> 00:10:43,780
Its graphite arrow shafts have steel razor blade tips,

195
00:10:43,781 --> 00:10:49,720
which fly at 300 feet per second and expand upon impact.

196
00:10:49,721 --> 00:10:52,720
In the field, it is virtually soundless.

197
00:10:52,721 --> 00:10:55,720
technology applied to a weapon from the Stone Age.

198
00:10:58,720 --> 00:11:03,720
From roughly 30,000 to 15,000 BC, the bow and arrow arose

199
00:11:03,721 --> 00:11:05,720
all over the world.

200
00:11:05,721 --> 00:11:09,720
Often called the third invention after fire and the wheel,

201
00:11:09,721 --> 00:11:11,720
it became our most powerful hunting weapon for thousands

202
00:11:11,721 --> 00:11:13,720
of years.

203
00:11:13,721 --> 00:11:15,720
The real value of the bow and arrow was the fact

204
00:11:15,721 --> 00:11:18,720
that no longer did man have to put himself directly at risk

205
00:11:18,721 --> 00:11:21,720
by shoving an instrument into an animal that was very, very

206
00:11:21,721 --> 00:11:23,020
an animal that was very, very close,

207
00:11:23,021 --> 00:11:24,560
sometimes a dangerous animal.

208
00:11:24,561 --> 00:11:27,060
So he was able to take animals from a greater distance,

209
00:11:27,061 --> 00:11:29,460
plus he was able to use greater velocity

210
00:11:29,461 --> 00:11:32,860
on the thrust of that projectile to get into an animal.

211
00:11:32,861 --> 00:11:35,000
So you could actually kill larger animals.

212
00:11:35,001 --> 00:11:37,060
So that meant more meat, more game,

213
00:11:37,061 --> 00:11:39,140
and more effective use of your time when you're hunting.

214
00:11:39,141 --> 00:11:41,840
So they were constructed out of very flexible materials,

215
00:11:41,841 --> 00:11:44,240
maybe wood from the yew tree or another tree

216
00:11:44,241 --> 00:11:45,320
that was very flexible.

217
00:11:45,321 --> 00:11:48,720
And of course, gut was used, dried gut that was flexible.

218
00:11:48,721 --> 00:11:51,280
And then very crude, rudimentary arrows were created

219
00:11:51,281 --> 00:11:53,180
with stone points.

220
00:11:53,280 --> 00:11:55,680
The bows were often carved out of tree limbs

221
00:11:55,780 --> 00:11:58,680
using stone tools.

222
00:11:58,780 --> 00:12:00,720
The discovery of the bow and arrow also

223
00:12:00,820 --> 00:12:03,420
led to a new kind of music.

224
00:12:03,520 --> 00:12:07,620
Cast yourself back in time 15,000 years,

225
00:12:07,720 --> 00:12:10,640
and you're sitting in a cave, and it's cold outside,

226
00:12:10,740 --> 00:12:15,340
and you're a hunter with a bow beside the fire.

227
00:12:15,440 --> 00:12:17,640
And maybe idly, you pick up your arrow

228
00:12:17,740 --> 00:12:20,280
and start to hit it against the string.

229
00:12:20,281 --> 00:12:23,180
a musical note is struck.

230
00:12:23,280 --> 00:12:26,480
Then you discover that if you put a little pressure

231
00:12:26,580 --> 00:12:28,720
on that string while you're striking it,

232
00:12:28,820 --> 00:12:31,320
you've changed the pitch.

233
00:12:31,420 --> 00:12:35,620
There's the birth of the stringed instrument.

234
00:12:35,720 --> 00:12:40,940
But it was definitely not used to serenade animals.

235
00:12:41,040 --> 00:12:45,640
By 7500 BC, the composite bow was invented,

236
00:12:45,740 --> 00:12:49,480
an instrument that used a variety of materials.

237
00:12:49,481 --> 00:12:54,780
Animal sinew was glued to the wood with fish-based adhesives.

238
00:12:54,781 --> 00:12:57,880
Animal bone, horn, and hides were grafted onto the center

239
00:12:57,881 --> 00:13:00,220
of the bow.

240
00:13:00,221 --> 00:13:02,620
This greatly increased the flexibility, range,

241
00:13:02,621 --> 00:13:06,020
and durability of the weapon.

242
00:13:06,021 --> 00:13:09,040
About 1200 AD, in the steppes of Asia,

243
00:13:09,041 --> 00:13:12,080
the Mongols came up with a major improvement.

244
00:13:12,081 --> 00:13:16,080
The shorter Mongol bows was one of the beginnings of what we

245
00:13:16,081 --> 00:13:21,080
would call a recurve bow with a counter bend already in the bow.

246
00:13:21,180 --> 00:13:24,880
So once you string it up and get it into the normal bow shape,

247
00:13:24,980 --> 00:13:26,620
it's much stronger of a bow.

248
00:13:26,720 --> 00:13:30,120
It has much more energy in the bow.

249
00:13:30,220 --> 00:13:32,940
The Mongols were expert horsemen.

250
00:13:33,040 --> 00:13:35,840
And the light, compact, and powerful recurve bow

251
00:13:35,940 --> 00:13:40,140
was the perfect weapon for their mobile hunting style.

252
00:13:40,240 --> 00:13:43,780
As firearms began to develop in 1300 AD,

253
00:13:43,781 --> 00:13:48,980
the technological evolution of archery virtually ceased.

254
00:13:49,080 --> 00:13:51,820
Ironically, interest in the weapon as a hunting tool

255
00:13:51,920 --> 00:13:55,320
was revived with the appearance from out of the bush

256
00:13:55,420 --> 00:13:59,120
of an exhausted and emaciated Indian in Oroville, California,

257
00:13:59,220 --> 00:14:03,140
on August 29, 1911.

258
00:14:03,240 --> 00:14:06,340
Ishii was the last surviving member of the Yahi,

259
00:14:06,440 --> 00:14:09,480
a Northern Californian tribe that had been exterminated

260
00:14:09,580 --> 00:14:11,980
by white settlers.

261
00:14:11,981 --> 00:14:15,520
As North America's last wild Indian, barely clothed and

262
00:14:15,620 --> 00:14:19,480
starving, he was quickly put under the care of anthropologists

263
00:14:19,580 --> 00:14:23,220
at a museum in the San Francisco Bay Area.

264
00:14:23,320 --> 00:14:26,320
One of Ishii's doctors was Dr. Saxon Pope.

265
00:14:26,420 --> 00:14:29,040
They became very good friends, and they spent a lot of time

266
00:14:29,140 --> 00:14:32,900
together in the woods where Ishii actually taught Dr.

267
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:37,240
Saxon Pope many of the skills he had in building a bow and arrow,

268
00:14:37,241 --> 00:14:42,040
game and understanding the skills necessary for hunting

269
00:14:42,041 --> 00:14:46,040
with a bow and arrow. The result was Pope's

270
00:14:46,041 --> 00:14:49,880
pivotal book, Hunting with a Bow and Arrow, which reestablished

271
00:14:49,881 --> 00:14:53,880
an interest in bow hunting. A young Californian, Doug

272
00:14:53,881 --> 00:14:57,400
Easton, was greatly influenced by the work and began tinkering

273
00:14:57,401 --> 00:15:01,800
with different equipment. The outcome was a 1930s invention

274
00:15:01,801 --> 00:15:07,240
that reinvigorated the sport, the aluminum arrow.

275
00:15:07,241 --> 00:15:10,240
Previously, arrows had always been made of wood and used

276
00:15:10,340 --> 00:15:15,240
feathers, which created drag that stabilized their flight.

277
00:15:15,340 --> 00:15:17,640
One of the challenges with wood arrows is that they're all a

278
00:15:17,740 --> 00:15:20,140
little bit different. My grandfather began experimenting

279
00:15:20,240 --> 00:15:24,140
with and developed and perfected the aluminum arrow, which

280
00:15:24,240 --> 00:15:28,140
created a much more consistent arrow, both in terms of weight,

281
00:15:28,240 --> 00:15:31,140
in terms of spine, which is the stiffness of the arrow. As the

282
00:15:31,240 --> 00:15:35,140
archer uses any number of arrows in his quiver, they're all the

283
00:15:35,141 --> 00:15:38,840
they're all the same and are going to hit the same spot.

284
00:15:38,841 --> 00:15:41,540
Today's arrows also generally use plastic instead

285
00:15:41,541 --> 00:15:45,280
of feathers for stabilization.

286
00:15:45,281 --> 00:15:49,780
In the 1950s, American Fred Bear created hunting bows of wood

287
00:15:49,781 --> 00:15:52,500
covered with fiberglass lamination.

288
00:15:52,501 --> 00:15:54,900
The new bows could be flexed thousands of times

289
00:15:54,901 --> 00:15:57,100
without cracking.

290
00:15:57,101 --> 00:15:59,840
They also had more power, accuracy,

291
00:15:59,841 --> 00:16:02,040
and were weatherproof.

292
00:16:02,041 --> 00:16:03,940
Several years later, another invention

293
00:16:03,941 --> 00:16:07,940
changed bow hunting forever. In the mid to late 60s, Hollis Allen

294
00:16:07,941 --> 00:16:10,940
created what is today called the compound bow. And this was a

295
00:16:10,941 --> 00:16:15,940
shorter bow than the traditional recurve bows. And by using a

296
00:16:15,941 --> 00:16:19,940
system of pulleys and wheels and cables, was able to create a

297
00:16:19,941 --> 00:16:23,940
much more powerful bow. An old long bow would maybe have to be

298
00:16:23,941 --> 00:16:27,940
a hundred pounds of strength that an archer would have to hold in

299
00:16:27,941 --> 00:16:31,940
order to propel an arrow at a great distance and accurately.

300
00:16:31,941 --> 00:16:35,240
The compound bows would allow that archer to have that same

301
00:16:35,241 --> 00:16:38,600
amount of energy, but only really have to hold 20 or 30

302
00:16:38,601 --> 00:16:40,540
pounds once he got the bow back.

303
00:16:40,900 --> 00:16:45,740
This opened up archery and hunting to a greater percentage

304
00:16:45,741 --> 00:16:46,580
of the population.

305
00:16:48,220 --> 00:16:50,800
While use of the bow and arrow has rebounded,

306
00:16:51,220 --> 00:16:53,480
one hunting tool has always been mandatory.

307
00:16:55,100 --> 00:16:58,980
In prehistoric times, hunting knives were used to hunt and

308
00:16:58,981 --> 00:16:59,681
kill.

309
00:16:59,682 --> 00:17:02,620
Today they are employed for post-kill activities,

310
00:17:02,621 --> 00:17:08,280
such as field dressing. Among the most famous are buck

311
00:17:08,281 --> 00:17:12,020
knives, manufactured in El Cajon, California.

312
00:17:12,021 --> 00:17:16,440
The company's first knife was made in 1902 by Hoyt Buck, a

313
00:17:16,441 --> 00:17:21,040
Kansas blacksmith. He ran across a method, he called

314
00:17:21,041 --> 00:17:24,040
it a secret process, but a method of actually heating the

315
00:17:24,041 --> 00:17:28,280
blade and quenching it in oil, and then it would come out much

316
00:17:28,281 --> 00:17:31,480
much more tough as opposed to being brittle,

317
00:17:31,680 --> 00:17:35,000
and that worked real good for a knife blade and for edge holding.

318
00:17:36,740 --> 00:17:39,600
For decades, Buck made each knife by hand,

319
00:17:40,100 --> 00:17:42,340
using worn-out files as his raw material

320
00:17:42,341 --> 00:17:44,780
and shaping them using a grinding wheel.

321
00:17:46,680 --> 00:17:50,180
In the early 1960s, Hoyt's son, Al,

322
00:17:50,181 --> 00:17:52,220
greatly increased the company's fortunes

323
00:17:52,221 --> 00:17:55,360
by creating the Model 110 Folding Hunter Knife,

324
00:17:55,820 --> 00:17:57,840
a collapsible knife of stainless steel.

325
00:17:58,280 --> 00:18:03,280
In 1963, we saw a need for a knife that would fold

326
00:18:03,380 --> 00:18:05,220
and be a safe knife.

327
00:18:05,320 --> 00:18:09,260
It would be enclosed so that the blade wouldn't cut anybody.

328
00:18:09,360 --> 00:18:11,800
If a guy fell off his horse hunting

329
00:18:11,900 --> 00:18:14,760
and he had a fixed blade, it could cut his leg.

330
00:18:14,860 --> 00:18:17,060
The knife also locked open,

331
00:18:17,160 --> 00:18:20,100
using a spring-operated metal bar that prevented the blade

332
00:18:20,200 --> 00:18:21,900
from closing on a person's fingers.

333
00:18:21,901 --> 00:18:22,900
years.

334
00:18:26,900 --> 00:18:29,900
Today's manufacturing of buck knives begins by punching a

335
00:18:29,901 --> 00:18:33,900
knife shape out of a steel blank.

336
00:18:33,901 --> 00:18:38,900
Thicker blanks are cut by a laser programmed by a computer.

337
00:18:38,901 --> 00:18:42,900
A double-disc machine then uniformly grinds the steel on

338
00:18:42,901 --> 00:18:46,900
each side to stringent specifications.

339
00:18:46,901 --> 00:18:50,900
A three-step heat treating process begins by hardening the

340
00:18:50,901 --> 00:18:57,020
in a 25-foot furnace at 2,000 degrees Fahrenheit.

341
00:18:57,021 --> 00:19:00,820
The blades are then plunged into a deep freeze of 120 degrees

342
00:19:00,821 --> 00:19:04,780
below zero, making them hard but brittle,

343
00:19:04,781 --> 00:19:09,260
then back into a tempering oven at 350 to 900 degrees

344
00:19:09,261 --> 00:19:12,980
to lower hardness but increase durability.

345
00:19:12,981 --> 00:19:15,800
A hollow grinding machine produces a thin edge,

346
00:19:15,801 --> 00:19:18,600
making the blades ready for final assembly and sharpening.

347
00:19:20,900 --> 00:19:24,400
While the evolution of knives spans millions of years,

348
00:19:24,401 --> 00:19:26,400
the real revolution in hunting gear

349
00:19:26,401 --> 00:19:28,900
is a result of the last 700 years.

350
00:19:32,400 --> 00:19:34,900
Modern aluminum arrows are so accurate

351
00:19:34,901 --> 00:19:36,900
that at a distance of 100 yards,

352
00:19:36,901 --> 00:19:41,400
their in-flight drift is no more than an inch.

353
00:19:41,401 --> 00:19:44,400
Hunting Gear will return on Modern Marvels.

354
00:19:44,401 --> 00:19:46,400
Marvels.

355
00:19:50,400 --> 00:19:55,400
We now return to hunting gear on Modern Marvels.

356
00:19:55,401 --> 00:19:57,400
The blazer rifle is one of today's most versatile

357
00:19:57,401 --> 00:20:00,400
firearms. It comes with five

358
00:20:00,401 --> 00:20:04,400
interchangeable barrels that can accommodate 20 different

359
00:20:04,401 --> 00:20:09,400
calibers of bullets. A hunter in a variety of terrain

360
00:20:09,401 --> 00:20:13,400
can routinely dismantle and change his gun to stalk widely

361
00:20:13,401 --> 00:20:18,500
to stock widely diverse types of game, from bear to deer

362
00:20:18,501 --> 00:20:21,200
to wildfowl.

363
00:20:21,201 --> 00:20:24,160
While today's guns pack a lethal punch,

364
00:20:24,161 --> 00:20:26,460
it all began with the invention of black powder

365
00:20:26,461 --> 00:20:29,960
in China about 900 AD.

366
00:20:29,961 --> 00:20:35,060
It was initially used in fireworks, toys, and for signaling.

367
00:20:35,061 --> 00:20:39,460
By 1300, Europeans were using it in hand cannons, which,

368
00:20:39,461 --> 00:20:43,400
when lit, fired projectiles through rudimentary pipes

369
00:20:43,401 --> 00:20:48,200
cast out of copper and iron.

370
00:20:48,201 --> 00:20:51,000
The first practical bullets soon followed.

371
00:20:51,001 --> 00:20:55,060
They were round and molded in lead.

372
00:20:55,061 --> 00:20:57,800
More ingenious inventions, like gun locks,

373
00:20:57,801 --> 00:21:01,580
were then created to ignite gunpowder.

374
00:21:01,581 --> 00:21:04,340
The wheel lock employed a hammer laced with soft iron

375
00:21:04,341 --> 00:21:07,960
pyrite, which was pressed onto a serrated iron wheel

376
00:21:07,961 --> 00:21:10,000
that turned with the aid of a spring

377
00:21:10,001 --> 00:21:13,600
when the trigger was pulled.

378
00:21:13,601 --> 00:21:18,300
The resulting sparks lit the charge.

379
00:21:18,301 --> 00:21:21,500
You could actually carry a gun, loaded, primed, cocked,

380
00:21:21,501 --> 00:21:25,900
and all you had to do was raise it and pull the trigger.

381
00:21:25,901 --> 00:21:29,500
In the early 1600s, one of Europe's first big game guns

382
00:21:29,501 --> 00:21:33,940
was the Jaeger rifle, a short, blunt, powerful firearm

383
00:21:33,941 --> 00:21:36,700
used for close hunting of stags and wild boar

384
00:21:36,701 --> 00:21:39,700
forests of Germany.

385
00:21:39,800 --> 00:21:43,240
Hunting was generally restricted to the landed gentry, who

386
00:21:43,340 --> 00:21:46,940
owned almost all the land and the game in Europe.

387
00:21:47,040 --> 00:21:49,440
Poaching was often a capital offense.

388
00:21:49,540 --> 00:21:51,940
When a lot of the Swiss and German immigrants

389
00:21:52,040 --> 00:21:54,460
came to the New World, they bought with them

390
00:21:54,560 --> 00:21:55,860
their Jaeger rifles.

391
00:21:55,960 --> 00:22:00,820
They quickly found out that the game that they hunted in America

392
00:22:00,920 --> 00:22:06,700
wasn't the same as the game in the European continent.

393
00:22:06,701 --> 00:22:09,700
The New World and its vast open spaces

394
00:22:09,800 --> 00:22:11,500
were a hunter's Eden.

395
00:22:11,600 --> 00:22:15,200
While hosting bounteous amounts of deer, moose, and elk,

396
00:22:15,300 --> 00:22:18,040
America was also teaming with smaller game,

397
00:22:18,140 --> 00:22:25,660
such as rabbit, turkey, and ducks.

398
00:22:25,760 --> 00:22:27,560
The new German immigrant gunsmiths

399
00:22:27,660 --> 00:22:30,760
realized they needed a lighter, more flexible gun

400
00:22:30,860 --> 00:22:32,800
that could fire with greater range.

401
00:22:32,900 --> 00:22:35,600
The result was the Kentucky rifle.

402
00:22:35,601 --> 00:22:39,480
They were longer, more delicate, better balanced,

403
00:22:40,120 --> 00:22:42,280
and generally speaking, more accurate.

404
00:22:42,600 --> 00:22:47,080
And you could have smaller bullets firing greater distances at greater power.

405
00:22:48,480 --> 00:22:52,840
By this time, the shotgun, which fired a cluster of pellets simultaneously,

406
00:22:53,060 --> 00:22:57,600
was also created, greatly aiding the killing of quicker, more elusive game.

407
00:22:58,860 --> 00:23:02,340
By the 18th century, guns became even more lethal

408
00:23:02,341 --> 00:23:05,200
by using conical bullets cast in lead.

409
00:23:05,600 --> 00:23:08,440
The new weapons and ammunition allowed early European

410
00:23:08,441 --> 00:23:10,400
immigrants to establish themselves

411
00:23:10,401 --> 00:23:12,900
on the growing American frontier, helped

412
00:23:12,901 --> 00:23:15,300
by the lack of hunting restrictions so common

413
00:23:15,301 --> 00:23:16,600
in their home countries.

414
00:23:16,601 --> 00:23:19,440
You could go out and shoot and hunt and feed your family

415
00:23:19,441 --> 00:23:21,140
with relative impunity.

416
00:23:21,141 --> 00:23:23,360
In the early 19th century, alchemists

417
00:23:23,361 --> 00:23:26,260
knew that when fulminative mercury was compressed,

418
00:23:26,261 --> 00:23:27,760
it detonated.

419
00:23:27,761 --> 00:23:29,560
Its combustible qualities were soon

420
00:23:29,561 --> 00:23:32,260
used to ignite firearms.

421
00:23:32,261 --> 00:23:36,160
In 1835, French gunmaker Casimir LeFauchot

422
00:23:36,260 --> 00:23:38,460
created the first practical application

423
00:23:38,560 --> 00:23:41,500
of a revolutionary invention.

424
00:23:41,600 --> 00:23:46,100
A self-contained cartridge of ammunition,

425
00:23:46,200 --> 00:23:50,220
a fulminative mercury percussion cap, a charge of powder,

426
00:23:50,320 --> 00:23:53,620
and the bullet were completely enveloped by a paper case

427
00:23:53,720 --> 00:23:57,320
inside a brass base that could be placed in a gun barrel.

428
00:23:57,321 --> 00:24:01,220
barrel.

429
00:24:01,320 --> 00:24:04,420
A pin rested on the percussion cap and protruded above the

430
00:24:04,520 --> 00:24:07,360
barrel.

431
00:24:07,460 --> 00:24:12,760
A falling hammer struck the pin, which exploded the cap,

432
00:24:12,860 --> 00:24:18,240
sparked the gunpowder, and fired the bullet.

433
00:24:18,340 --> 00:24:21,280
In addition, the cartridge was loaded by cracking open the

434
00:24:21,380 --> 00:24:24,680
breach of the gun, a 16th-century invention that

435
00:24:24,681 --> 00:24:29,180
was a vastly quicker and simpler method than muzzle loading.

436
00:24:29,181 --> 00:24:31,780
The big advantage in a breech loader to a hunter

437
00:24:31,781 --> 00:24:33,920
is that he's got fast follow-up shots.

438
00:24:33,921 --> 00:24:36,640
He doesn't have to change the position of his rifle,

439
00:24:36,641 --> 00:24:39,120
break out all the accessories that go with it,

440
00:24:39,121 --> 00:24:42,120
the powder, the caps, the flints, all the other things

441
00:24:42,121 --> 00:24:42,920
that go with it.

442
00:24:42,921 --> 00:24:46,240
He simply cranks that gun open, shoves in another cartridge,

443
00:24:46,241 --> 00:24:49,080
closes it up, and he's ready to fire at his game

444
00:24:49,081 --> 00:24:50,740
or protect his life, whatever.

445
00:24:50,741 --> 00:24:54,240
But it's rapid fire, rapid controlled fire,

446
00:24:54,241 --> 00:24:57,780
which is what brings meat to the table.

447
00:24:57,781 --> 00:25:00,280
One of the first guns to carry multiple cartridges

448
00:25:00,281 --> 00:25:03,200
allowing rapid firing without having to reload

449
00:25:03,201 --> 00:25:08,540
was the 1873 Winchester lever action repeating rifle.

450
00:25:08,541 --> 00:25:10,700
It was a key tool used by the rural hunter

451
00:25:10,701 --> 00:25:13,400
in the settling of the American West.

452
00:25:13,401 --> 00:25:16,040
The gun user would place one finger on the trigger

453
00:25:16,041 --> 00:25:19,340
and the other three fingers on a lower lever that, when cranked,

454
00:25:19,341 --> 00:25:22,640
would move each cartridge into place after firing.

455
00:25:22,641 --> 00:25:27,340
Each firearm held up to 15 cartridges, lined up horizontally one behind the other.

456
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:35,560
The ability to carry a lot of ammunition in your rifle was a great boon, not only to frontiersmen of all sorts,

457
00:25:35,640 --> 00:25:42,440
but especially to hunters, because if they're shooting at running game, they've got fast follow-up shots.

458
00:25:42,540 --> 00:25:46,900
They can fire one shot, and even if that animal is hit and doesn't go down right away,

459
00:25:47,260 --> 00:25:50,260
they can lever quickly, take aim again.

460
00:25:50,261 --> 00:25:52,500
And a lot of times, the levering was so easy,

461
00:25:52,600 --> 00:25:54,400
they could lever right from the shoulder

462
00:25:54,500 --> 00:25:57,200
and not lose much of their target or their sight picture.

463
00:26:01,000 --> 00:26:03,400
All this firepower would lead to a dark chapter

464
00:26:03,500 --> 00:26:05,400
in the history of the West.

465
00:26:05,500 --> 00:26:10,020
Around the mid-19th century, the Sharps rifle was invented,

466
00:26:10,120 --> 00:26:13,420
the firearm most responsible for the virtual extermination

467
00:26:13,520 --> 00:26:16,260
of an estimated 60 million buffalo

468
00:26:16,261 --> 00:26:20,660
and the collapse of many Native Americans' way of life.

469
00:26:20,760 --> 00:26:23,860
In the 1870s, market hunters, feeding the frenzy

470
00:26:23,960 --> 00:26:27,000
for buffalo robes and coats on the East Coast,

471
00:26:27,100 --> 00:26:31,200
began a slaughter that had no legal restrictions.

472
00:26:31,300 --> 00:26:33,320
And the .45 caliber Sharps rifle

473
00:26:33,420 --> 00:26:36,220
was the perfect big game killing tool.

474
00:26:36,320 --> 00:26:39,660
It was a big, powerful, single shot rifle.

475
00:26:39,760 --> 00:26:42,560
And the buffalo hunters liked it because they could place one

476
00:26:42,660 --> 00:26:44,260
shot into an animal.

477
00:26:44,261 --> 00:26:46,660
And usually that would down that animal.

478
00:26:47,000 --> 00:26:50,620
Generally, because buffalo are a matriarchal society,

479
00:26:51,080 --> 00:26:53,260
the buffalo hunters would look to see

480
00:26:53,261 --> 00:26:56,180
which seemed to be the lead female running the herd.

481
00:26:56,820 --> 00:26:59,700
And they would fire at that animal, drop that animal.

482
00:27:00,040 --> 00:27:01,440
Then as the others would mill around,

483
00:27:01,540 --> 00:27:03,920
they would look for the animal that seemed to be in charge

484
00:27:03,921 --> 00:27:06,480
or taking charge next, and they would drop that one.

485
00:27:06,880 --> 00:27:09,480
And because buffalo were very bewildered,

486
00:27:09,560 --> 00:27:11,200
they didn't understand what this was,

487
00:27:11,720 --> 00:27:13,240
the buffalo hunter could sit there,

488
00:27:13,241 --> 00:27:19,900
generally for hours, and they would shoot as much as their skinners could skin in a single day

489
00:27:19,901 --> 00:27:26,020
without leaving one position. After only a dozen years,

490
00:27:26,021 --> 00:27:28,540
the giant herds were almost completely gone.

491
00:27:31,820 --> 00:27:37,217
The Sharps rifle used a 500-grain bullet, about one-tenth of a pound.

492
00:27:37,218 --> 00:27:38,860
But British colonials

493
00:27:38,861 --> 00:27:44,200
in Africa in the 1860s were creating even larger weapons.

494
00:27:44,201 --> 00:27:48,300
Their own elephant guns, huge double-barreled firearms

495
00:27:48,301 --> 00:27:50,600
for hunting big game.

496
00:27:50,601 --> 00:27:53,420
These behemoths used bullets that were up to three times

497
00:27:53,421 --> 00:27:58,020
the size of the buffalo gun bullets.

498
00:27:58,021 --> 00:28:00,000
When the British took over most of the earth,

499
00:28:00,001 --> 00:28:03,000
they also took over a lot of hunting grounds, especially

500
00:28:03,001 --> 00:28:04,860
in Africa and in India.

501
00:28:04,861 --> 00:28:07,320
You're dealing with some pretty serious game.

502
00:28:07,321 --> 00:28:12,160
You're dealing with a Cape buffalo or a rhinoceros or an elephant.

503
00:28:12,161 --> 00:28:16,620
So you wanted the most powerful, most reliable firearms that you could get.

504
00:28:16,621 --> 00:28:21,340
In the middle of the 19th century, this took the form of the double rifle.

505
00:28:21,341 --> 00:28:26,340
You had two shots rather than one, which gave you kind of an extra backup.

506
00:28:26,341 --> 00:28:29,100
You want a big bullet to knock them down.

507
00:28:29,101 --> 00:28:34,380
This meant you're shooting a bullet or a round ball that weighs a quarter or a half a pound.

508
00:28:34,381 --> 00:28:36,780
And the recoil on these things was considerable.

509
00:28:36,781 --> 00:28:37,940
It's a miserable experience.

510
00:28:37,941 --> 00:28:41,760
It causes as much damage on the back end as it is on the front.

511
00:28:41,920 --> 00:28:42,960
You don't want to shoot it a lot.

